hi there steve  i've been doing a couple of hours here  of reading korean on link and uh  been doing some listening while making  breakfast and cleaning up but today i'm  going to talk about listening and how do  we improve our listening comprehension  and i'm doing this video because i had a  question  from brazil from francois  who says he's got to go to the states on  some kind of a  exchange he's a  medical specialist and wants to be fully  proficient in english when he gets there  okay here's my answer  listening comprehension first of all to  me is the fundamental skill if you can  understand very well when people speak  when you listen watch movies listen to  audio books other people are speaking  amongst each other that's the ultimate  skill once you have that you will learn  to speak i don't believe people who say  i understand very well but i can't speak  i haven't met people who are in that  category most people have trouble  understanding  so how do you work on your listing  comprehension first of all you have to  listen a lot because why do we have  trouble understanding either we don't  hear the words or they go by so quickly  that we can't make meaning of them or we  simply don't have the words we don't  know what the words mean so it's either  that we can't hear the words or we don't  know the words so i think you have to  have a two-pronged approach  and that's essentially what i'm doing in  korean i don't have enough words and  when sometimes when i hear them i don't  recognize them so i have to do a lot of  listening  i try the ideal scenario is that i have  the text the transcript for what i'm  listening to  but that's not always the case and so  you have to like so much in language  learning you have to be prepared to deal  with less than ideal conditions so for  example  when i read something  on link  in korean i use  to speech function now at link it's  automatically on so that every word that  i save every phrase that i save i hear  the text to speech so as i'm reading  through  my text i'm going from text to speech to  text to speech to text to speech so it's  not as if i'm listening to the whole  thing but i'm listening to a lot of key  words  that helps me  once i've gone through the text once  i've saved a bunch of words looked them  up heard the text to speech when i then  go and listen to it all it's easier it's  easier because i'm more familiar with  the subject matter which is so important  to comprehension being familiar with the  subject matter i've looked up some of  the words that i didn't know i have  highlighted and therefore listen to some  of the phrases that i have trouble with  all of this helps me when i then go to  listen but ultimately you have to listen  a lot now you can do the same with text  for which you don't have audio because  even though you won't be able to listen  to it afterwards you can still go  through it saving words therefore  increasing the number of words that you  know  listening to the audio even though it's  artificial speech audio of the words and  phrases  and then you may in fact be listening to  something else another text on a  somewhat similar subject so it's not as  ideal as having the audio for the that  at least the transcript for the audio  that you're listening to or vice versa  but it still helps so  you know it's just as with so much in  language learning it's just time we have  the impression that we're not improving  until suddenly we realize that we've  improved so my only advice here to  franzoldo is to continue doing what  you're doing use link  listen a lot  use the text-to-speech function at link  get more and more familiar just just  you know immerse your brain in english  and you will find that you will improve  and of course as long as you are in  brazil and you have less opportunity to  speak you won't be able to speak as well  as you would like however if you spend a  lot of time with listening and reading  and building up your vocabulary and  getting the brain familiar with hearing  english when you arrive in the united  states for your fellowship or whatever  exchange program you have there within  two weeks you'll be just flying in  english i guarantee it and good luck bye  for now

